.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt INOTIFY_INIT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm inotify_init ,
.Nm inotify_init1 ,
.Nm inotify_add_watch ,
.Nm inotify_rm_watch
.Nd a descriptor that reports changes to files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/inotify.h
.Ft int
.Fn inotify_init "void"
.Ft int
.Fn inotify_init1 "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn inotify_add_watch "int fd" "const char *path" "uint32_t mask"
.Ft int
.Fn inotify_rm_watch "int fd" "int wd"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn inotify_init1 ,
number 332 on i386 and 294 on x86-64, returns a new inotify instance: a descriptor on which the
kernel queues an event when a watched file changes.
.Fa flags
may have
.Dv IN_NONBLOCK
and
.Dv IN_CLOEXEC .
.Fn inotify_init ,
number 291 on i386 and 253 on x86-64, is the same call with no flags.
.Pp
.Fn inotify_add_watch ,
number 292 on i386 and 254 on x86-64, watches the file
.Fa path
names for the events of
.Fa mask
and returns the watch's descriptor, which each event of it carries.
The path is followed through a final symbolic link unless
.Fa mask
has
.Dv IN_DONT_FOLLOW ,
must name a directory under
.Dv IN_ONLYDIR ,
and names a file the caller may read.
A second watch of the same file by the same instance changes the first
and returns its descriptor: its mask is replaced, or with
.Dv IN_MASK_ADD
added to, and with
.Dv IN_MASK_CREATE
the call fails instead.
A watch with
.Dv IN_ONESHOT
goes after its first event.
.Dv IN_EXCL_UNLINK
is accepted and changes nothing.
Descriptors count up from 1.
.Fn inotify_rm_watch ,
number 293 on i386 and 255 on x86-64, removes the watch
.Fa wd .
.Pp
A watch of a file reports these events of the file itself:
.Bl -tag -width IN_CLOSE_NOWRITE
.It Dv IN_ACCESS
A read of it.
.It Dv IN_MODIFY
A write, a truncation, or an open with
.Dv O_TRUNC
of a file the open did not create.
.It Dv IN_ATTRIB
A change of its mode, owner or times, or of its count of links.
.It Dv IN_OPEN , IN_CLOSE_WRITE , IN_CLOSE_NOWRITE
An
.Xr open 2 ,
and the last close of what it opened, for writing or not.
.It Dv IN_MOVE_SELF
A rename of it.
.It Dv IN_DELETE_SELF
The removal of its last link, or of the directory.
.El
.Pp
A watch of a directory reports the same of the directory, and these of
the names in it, each with the name:
.Dv IN_CREATE
for a file, directory, link or node made in it,
.Dv IN_DELETE
for one removed,
.Dv IN_MOVED_FROM
and
.Dv IN_MOVED_TO
for a rename out of it and into it, the two events of one rename with
the same cookie, and the events of a file opened by a name in it:
.Dv IN_ACCESS ,
.Dv IN_MODIFY ,
.Dv IN_OPEN
and the closes.
An event about a directory has
.Dv IN_ISDIR .
When a watch goes, by
.Fn inotify_rm_watch ,
by its file's removal, or by the
.Xr umount 2
of its file's file system, which first queues
.Dv IN_UNMOUNT ,
an
.Dv IN_IGNORED
event is queued for it.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
returns as many whole events as fit: a
.Vt struct inotify_event
of 16 bytes, the watch's descriptor, the mask, the cookie and the length
of the name that follows, padded with NULs to a multiple of 16.
A read with no room for the next event fails with
.Er EINVAL .
An empty queue waits, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
with
.Dv IN_NONBLOCK .
An event the same as the last one queued and not yet read is not queued
again, so a file written many times between two reads is one
.Dv IN_MODIFY .
An instance queues 16384 events at most; the next is lost, and one
.Dv IN_Q_OVERFLOW
event with descriptor \-1 says so.
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report the descriptor readable while an event is queued, and the
.Dv FIONREAD
request of
.Xr ioctl 2
gives the bytes a read of the whole queue returns.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:inotify .
.Pp
A user may have 128 instances and 8192 watches, the limits
.Pa /proc/sys/fs/inotify
reports.
.Pp
Where Linux differs: a file is gone for its watches when its last link
is removed, where Linux waits for its last close; the events of a file
reach the watch of its directory only through a descriptor opened by a
name in that directory while a watch existed, so a change of a file's
mode or owner by path, and a file opened before any watch existed, are
reported to the file's own watches alone; and a watch is found by the
file's inode number, which a rename changes on a FAT volume, so a watch
of a file there does not follow it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn inotify_init ,
.Fn inotify_init1
and
.Fn inotify_add_watch
return a descriptor, and
.Fn inotify_rm_watch
returns 0; on failure each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
The caller may not read the file, or search a directory of the path.
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open.
.It Er EEXIST
.Dv IN_MASK_CREATE
of a file the instance watches.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
has another bit;
.Fa fd
is not an instance;
.Fa mask
has no event, or both
.Dv IN_MASK_ADD
and
.Dv IN_MASK_CREATE ;
.Fa wd
is not a watch of the instance; or a read was too small for the next
event.
.It Er ELOOP
The path has too many symbolic links.
.It Er EMFILE
The user has 128 instances, or the process has no free descriptor below
its limit.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path is too long.
.It Er ENOENT
The path names no file.
.It Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the instance or the watch.
.It Er ENOSPC
The user has 8192 watches.
.It Er ENOTDIR
.Dv IN_ONLYDIR
of a file that is not a directory, or a component of the path is not a
directory.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr epoll 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
